; DIVMOD AND COMPARISON ON AN ARBITRARY-PRECISION VALUE THAT IS INTERNAL.
;
; This is what the limb library's two additions are FOR, written the way they
; will be used rather than as isolated calls. `(% (/ x 100000000) 100000000)` is
; one step of DECIMAL RENDERING — printing a big value is repeatedly taking the
; quotient and the remainder by a power of ten, and each remainder is a group of
; digits — and `(> x (scaled 4))` is a bignum controlling a branch, which was not
; expressible at all on a target storing its big values as limbs.
;
; AND THE BIG VALUE IS DECLARED ON A HELPER, WHICH IS THE POINT. `scaled` says
; its result is arbitrary-precision; `run` returns an ordinary `int` and never
; mentions a big type. Reduction inlines `scaled` away, so the declaration is
; gone before the representation is selected — and until `the` existed this
; program was REFUSED, with four operations unprovable because nothing told the
; solver the accumulator was meant to be exact.
;
; The interval analysis cannot supply what the declaration does: it reports
; `[-inf, +inf]` for `(* acc 999983)` even though the trip count is a constant 6.
; So this is not a precision gap that a better domain closes — a factorial's
; bound is not expressible in an interval domain at all. It is INTENT, and intent
; needs a carrier that survives reduction.
;
;
; WINDOWS RUNS THIS NOW, and it did not until 2026-09-08. The refusal was
; `x64.movb has more spilled operands than there are scratch registers`: the
; scratch pool is two registers, a table store has three operands, and reduction
; inlines `run` at every call site so this procedure has more live limb buffers
; than the value pool holds. It reproduced at ONE input, so it was program SIZE
; rather than any construct here.
;
; The fix is that a template with more spilled operands than scratch now BORROWS
; a value register, saved to a frame slot for the length of the instruction. A
; value register is callee-saved, so a template that makes a call gets it back;
; and a frame slot rather than a push, because a push shifts the 16-byte
; alignment a callee's own spill depends on.
;
; `limb-subdiv` stays, and is not made redundant by this: it writes the same
; loops by hand and returns ordinary integers, so it covers two shapes on x86
; that nothing else does — a conditional inside an `again` argument, and a loop
; counting down.
;
; THE RESULT TYPE IS LOAD-BEARING. `a % k` with 0 <= a and k a machine word is
; under k, so the answer fits the portable window whatever a's magnitude —
; arithmetic, not representation. Before `big%-small` existed the fixed-limb rung
; returned a word here and the host rungs returned a bignum, so this exact
; signature was accepted on one and refused on the other: selecting a
; representation would have changed which programs are LEGAL.
;
;   x(n)   = (n+7) * 999983^6            -- about 10^36, 123 bits
;   run(n) = (x / 10^8) % 10^8  +  (10^12 if x > x(4) else 0)
;
; big-repr: both

; inputs: 0 5
; expect: 96453661 1000036777705

(export run)

; NOT EXPORTED, so reduction inlines it and nothing of this signature reaches the
; residual except through `the`.
(sig scaled ((n (int 0 50))) (int 0 (pow 2 200)))
(def scaled (n)
  (loop ((acc (+ n 7)) (i 0))
    (>= i 6)  acc
    else      (again (* acc 999983) (+ i 1))))

(sig run ((n (int 0 50))) int)
(def run (n)
  (let (scaled n) (fn (x)
    (+ (% (/ x 100000000) 100000000)
       (if (> x (scaled 4)) 1000000000000 0)))))
